<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karatay Madrasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1251</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Karatay Medrese</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karatay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a> is a Seljuk theological college in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, founded in 1251 by the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-karatay">Jalal al-Din Karatay</a>. It is known for its marble portal and for the turquoise and black tile mosaic covering the dome of its central hall. The building now houses a museum of Seljuk and later ceramic tiles, including tiles from the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubadabad-palace">Kubadabad</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karatay-madrasa.jpg" alt="Karatay Madrasa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konya_Karatay_Ceramics_Museum_2829.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
